This file contains more detailed doctests for the stdnum.kz.iin module. It
tries to validate a number of numbers that have been found online.

>>> from stdnum.kz import iin
>>> from stdnum.exceptions import *


Tests for some corner cases.

>>> iin.validate('921029 35085 1')
'921029350851'
>>> iin.is_valid('921029350851')
True
>>> iin.is_valid('921029350852')
False
>>> iin.validate('921029350852')
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidChecksum: ...
>>> iin.validate('921529350859') # wrong date
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidComponent: ...
>>> iin.validate('921029950850') # wrong gender code
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidComponent: ...
>>> iin.validate('921029351840') # wrong serial registration number
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidComponent: ...
>>> iin.validate('991231623804') # future date
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidComponent: ...
>>> iin.validate('123')
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidLength: ...
>>> iin.validate('92102935085A')
Traceback (most recent call last):
    ...
InvalidFormat: ...
>>> iin.get_gender('840621350384')
'M'
>>> iin.get_gender('850830402160')
'F'
>>> print(iin.get_gender('960206001028'))
None
>>> iin.get_birth_date('960206101024')
datetime.date(1896, 2, 6)
>>> iin.get_birth_date('960206001028')
datetime.date(1996, 2, 6)
>>> iin.get_birth_date('000206001029')
datetime.date(2000, 2, 6)


